# Next.js

Builds on `code-javascript` and `code-react`. Prefer the project's Next.js
major (App Router vs Pages) — do not migrate routers or add a second
framework unless asked.

## App Router (default for new work when the repo already uses it)

- Default to **Server Components**; add `"use client"` only for browser
  state, effects, or event handlers
- Keep client components small; push data fetching and secrets to the server
- Do not import server-only modules (fs, secret env, DB) into client components
- `page.tsx` / `layout.tsx`: keep them thin — compose UI and fetch helpers

## Data and caching

- Fetch on the server when possible; use the project's cache/revalidate
  patterns (`fetch` cache options, `revalidatePath` / `revalidateTag`, …)
- Do not expose secret env vars to the client — only `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` (or the
  project's public prefix) belongs in browser bundles
- Mutations: prefer Server Actions or existing API routes; validate input and
  auth on the server every time

## Routing and navigation

- Use the project's navigation helpers (`next/link`, `next/navigation`) —
  not full page reloads for in-app transitions
- Dynamic segments: validate params; do not trust them for authz alone
- Prefer colocated `loading.tsx` / `error.tsx` when the app already uses them

## Pages Router (legacy)

- When the repo is still Pages Router, match its `getServerSideProps` /
  `getStaticProps` / API route patterns — do not invent a hybrid without an
  ask
- Keep API routes thin; shared logic in modules both pages and tests can import

## Assets and performance

- Use `next/image` (or the project's image helper) for bitmaps when already
  adopted
- Avoid shipping huge client bundles; dynamic-import heavy, client-only
  widgets when the project already does

## Tests

- Follow the repo's Next/React test setup; ship tests with behavior changes
  when a runner exists
